BT /F1 10 Tf 50 720 Td (IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
AT NEW DELHI

CASE NO: Crl.A. No. 589 of 2026
CNR NUMBER: SCIN010056782026
DECISION DATE: 2026-02-10
YEAR: 2026

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Justice Vikram Nath & Justice Prasanna B. Varale

PARTIES:
Rakesh Sharma
... Appellant\(s\) / Petitioner\(s\)
Versus
Central Bureau of Investigation \(CBI\)
... Respondent\(s\) / State

STATUTORY PROVISIONS & ACTS REFERRED:
Indian Evidence Act 1872, Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam 2023, Information Technology Act 2000

SECTIONS & CLAUSES UNDER CONSIDERATION:
IEA Section 65B, BSA Section 61, IT Act Section 43

KEY LEGAL DOMAINS & TOPICS:
Electronic Evidence, Section 65B Certificate, Call Detail Records, Secondary Evidence, Arjun Panditrao

================================================================================
                                   JUDGMENT
================================================================================

1. FACTUAL MATRIX:
Conviction under Prevention of Corruption Act was solely based on WhatsApp audio recordings and Call Detail Records produced without mandatory Section 65B certificate.

2. ISSUES FRAMED FOR ADJUDICATION:
1. Whether electronic evidence in the form of Call Detail Records can be admitted without certificate under Section 65B\(4\) IEA / Section 61 BSA?

3. STATUTORY FRAMEWORK & PRECEDENTS:
Arjun Panditrao Khotkar v. Kailash Kushanrao Gorantyal \(Constitution Bench\): Section 65B\(4\) certificate is a condition precedent to admissibility of secondary electronic evidence.

4. RATIO DECIDENDI:
Production of Section 65B certificate is mandatory condition precedent for admissibility of secondary electronic records; absence of certificate renders evidence inadmissible.

5. FINAL DISPOSITION & ORDER:
Criminal Appeal Allowed; Conviction set aside for violation of electronic evidence certification under Section 65B of Evidence Act.

Decreed and ordered accordingly.

Hon'ble Justice Vikram Nath & Justice Prasanna B. Varale
Supreme Court of India
